{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794544646781,"sessionID":"ses_e2cd19d92ffeD6u5HsfuPaRRpf","part":{"partID":"prt_1d3323909001N8cNttxRBPzPz3","sessionID":"ses_e2cd19d92ffeD6u5HsfuPaRRpf","messageID":"msg_1d3319fa6001NqC7FlOay9bj88","type":"tool","id":"call_635a185c874f4c51948cd689","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md, lines 1-125\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Skill hub routing\"\n3: description: \"A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"hub routing\"\n6:   - \"parent hub\"\n7:   - \"nested mode\"\n8:   - \"one advisor identity\"\n9:   - \"two-stage routing\"\n10:   - \"stage one and stage two\"\n11:   - \"adding a mode to a hub\"\n12:   - \"mode registry\"\n13:   - \"routerSignals\"\n14:   - \"tieBreak\"\n15:   - \"hub SKILL.md\"\n16:   - \"is it wired\"\n17:   - \"did it actually route\"\n18:   - \"the mode is registered but nothing routes\"\n19:   - \"advisor does not surface\"\n20:   - \"routing class metadata\"\n21:   - \"refactoring a skill\"\n22:   - \"mode not routed\"\n23:   - \"hub registry\"\n24: importance_tier: normal\n25: contextType: reference\n26: version: 1.0.0.3\n27: ---\n28: \n29: # Rule: Skill hub routing\n30: \n31: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load it before wiring, rewiring or removing a mode in a parent-hub skill, or before reporting that one routes. It sits in the routing carve-out of section 4: verifying wiring you changed is In, selecting a route is not.\n32: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n33: \n34: ## Fires when\n35: \n36: - Adding, renaming or removing a mode in a hub that carries `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json`.\n37: - Creating a new skill that will live under a hub, or refactoring one that already does.\n38: - Editing `graph-metadata.json`, `hub-router.json`, a root `ROUTER.md`, a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md`, or a hub's `SKILL.md` mode table.\n39: - Reporting that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated.\n40: - Running a per-hub gate and quoting its result.\n41: \n42: ## The rule\n43: \n44: **Report a mode as routed only after checking both stages against the hub you actually changed.**\n45: \n46: The failure is quiet: the entry exists, every validator is green, and nothing reaches the mode.\n47: \n48: ---\n49: \n50: ## 1. THE TWO STAGES\n51: \n52: The advisor scores the **hub**. Most nested modes are `routingClass: \"metadata\"`, resolved by hub membership, with no advisor entry of their own, and a second skill-shaped `graph-metadata.json` below the root is rejected. A minority are not: `lexical` and `alias-fold` modes carry their own advisor entries and projection maps, and `command-bridge` modes route by command surface. **Read the class before assuming which path applies.**\n53: \n54: So routing runs in two stages, and each can pass while the other is broken:\n55: \n56: | Stage | Owner | Picks |\n57: |-------|-------|-------|\n58: | One | the advisor, reading the hub's `graph-metadata.json` | the hub |\n59: | Two | the hub's `hub-router.json` and root `ROUTER.md` | the mode, then its leaves |\n60: \n61: Every hub and every mode packet owns the `ROUTER.md` in its own folder. The hub root's file is the stage-two control document, and a packet's file holds that packet's machine router under its `## MACHINE ROUTER` heading. A packet's `SKILL.md` keeps only short routing prose and a link to it, so a router edit belongs in the `ROUTER.md`, never in `SKILL.md`.\n62: \n63: A `metadata` mode's vocabulary reaches the advisor **only** through the hub's `graph-metadata.json`. A `lexical` or `alias-fold` mode also needs its projection maps and scorer entries. A stage-one hit with no stage-two intent, or the reverse, means the two disagree about the same phrasing: fix both sides, not the one that happens to be failing.\n64: \n65: **The failure this prevents:** concluding the advisor is broken because it does not surface a nested mode by name. It is not broken. It was never going to.\n66: \n67: ---\n68: \n69: ## 2. REGISTERED IS NOT ROUTED\n70: \n71: Before saying a mode is integrated, confirm every surface carries it. The authoritative list, with what each one breaks, is section 7 of `parent-skills-nested-packets.md`, which `sk-doc` routes to.\n72: \n73: The two most often missed:\n74: \n75: - The hub's own `SKILL.md` mode table. It is the discovery surface a runtime shows when the advisor is unreachable.\n76: - The runtime command mirrors. A command present in one runtime tree and absent from three is unreachable from those three.\n77: \n78: **A green gate is not integration.** The per-hub gate covers some surfaces and asserts only presence, never reachability. Several surfaces have no gate at all.\n79: \n80: **The failure this prevents:** reporting \"the registries are correct\" after checking that entries exist rather than that the policy is complete, or reading a green gate as proof a request can reach the mode.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 3. CHECK THE HUB YOU CHANGED\n85: \n86: A per-hub gate run without its hub argument reports on whichever hub it defaults to. The output is green, detailed and about something else.\n87: \n88: Pass the hub path explicitly to the repository's parent-skill checker, and read the\n89: subject line before the verdict. Then replay both stages with a real request for the mode. The commands are in the reference above.\n90: \n91: **The failure this prevents:** a green run that certifies a hub you never touched. This is the green-run-lies case from [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) with a specific, repeatable shape.\n92: \n93: ---\n94: \n95: ## 4. KEEP THE ALIAS NARROW\n96: \n97: An alias earns its place by catching a request for **this** mode. A term that also matches unrelated work (`rule file`, `config`, `template`) captures traffic the hub cannot serve.\n98: \n99: Replay each new alias against a plausible out-of-domain phrase before shipping it.\n100: \n101: ---\n102: \n103: ## 5. WHERE THE DETAIL LIVES\n104: \n105: This rule carries the discipline. The mechanics belong to the skills that own them.\n106: \n107: | Question | Read |\n108: |----------|------|\n109: | What must a new mode land on, and in what order | `parent-skills-nested-packets.md` §7 |\n110: | Hub doctrine, one identity, the `routingClass` table, where each `ROUTER.md` lives | `parent-skills-nested-packets.md` §1-2 |\n111: | Which metadata file is required or forbidden at which root | `skill-root-metadata-contract.md` |\n112: | How the advisor scores, and its regression corpus | the skill-advisor system skill |\n113: | Authoring or repairing a hub | `sk-doc` → `sk-create-skill` |\n114: \n115: ---\n116: \n117: ## 6. SELF-CHECK\n118: \n119: - [ ] I know which stage I changed, and I checked the other one.\n120: - [ ] Every surface in the reference list carries the mode, not just the registry.\n121: - [ ] The hub's own `SKILL.md` names the mode.\n122: - [ ] I passed the hub path to the per-hub gate and read its subject line.\n123: - [ ] I replayed a real request through both stages, not just a validator.\n124: - [ ] Each new alias was replayed against an out-of-domain phrase.\n125: - [ ] Nothing I reported as routed was inferred from a registry entry alone.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md, lines 1-125\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Skill hub routing\"\n3: description: \"A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"hub routing\"\n6:   - \"parent hub\"\n7:   - \"nested mode\"\n8:   - \"one advisor identity\"\n9:   - \"two-stage routing\"\n10:   - \"stage one and stage two\"\n11:   - \"adding a mode to a hub\"\n12:   - \"mode registry\"\n13:   - \"routerSignals\"\n14:   - \"tieBreak\"\n15:   - \"hub SKILL.md\"\n16:   - \"is it wired\"\n17:   - \"did it actually route\"\n18:   - \"the mode is registered but nothing routes\"\n19:   - \"advisor does not surface\"\n20:   - \"routing class metadata\"\n21:   - \"refactoring a skill\"\n22:   - \"mode not routed\"\n23:   - \"hub registry\"\n24: importance_tier: normal\n25: contextType: reference\n26: version: 1.0.0.3\n27: ---\n28: \n29: # Rule: Skill hub routing\n30: \n31: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load it before wiring, rewiring or removing a mode in a parent-hub skill, or before reporting that one routes. It sits in the routing carve-out of section 4: verifying wiring you changed is In, selecting a route is not.\n32: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n33: \n34: ## Fires when\n35: \n36: - Adding, renaming or removing a mode in a hub that carries `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json`.\n37: - Creating a new skill that will live under a hub, or refactoring one that already does.\n38: - Editing `graph-metadata.json`, `hub-router.json`, a root `ROUTER.md`, a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md`, or a hub's `SKILL.md` mode table.\n39: - Reporting that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated.\n40: - Running a per-hub gate and quoting its result.\n41: \n42: ## The rule\n43: \n44: **Report a mode as routed only after checking both stages against the hub you actually changed.**\n45: \n46: The failure is quiet: the entry exists, every validator is green, and nothing reaches the mode.\n47: \n48: ---\n49: \n50: ## 1. THE TWO STAGES\n51: \n52: The advisor scores the **hub**. Most nested modes are `routingClass: \"metadata\"`, resolved by hub membership, with no advisor entry of their own, and a second skill-shaped `graph-metadata.json` below the root is rejected. A minority are not: `lexical` and `alias-fold` modes carry their own advisor entries and projection maps, and `command-bridge` modes route by command surface. **Read the class before assuming which path applies.**\n53: \n54: So routing runs in two stages, and each can pass while the other is broken:\n55: \n56: | Stage | Owner | Picks |\n57: |-------|-------|-------|\n58: | One | the advisor, reading the hub's `graph-metadata.json` | the hub |\n59: | Two | the hub's `hub-router.json` and root `ROUTER.md` | the mode, then its leaves |\n60: \n61: Every hub and every mode packet owns the `ROUTER.md` in its own folder. The hub root's file is the stage-two control document, and a packet's file holds that packet's machine router under its `## MACHINE ROUTER` heading. A packet's `SKILL.md` keeps only short routing prose and a link to it, so a router edit belongs in the `ROUTER.md`, never in `SKILL.md`.\n62: \n63: A `metadata` mode's vocabulary reaches the advisor **only** through the hub's `graph-metadata.json`. A `lexical` or `alias-fold` mode also needs its projection maps and scorer entries. A stage-one hit with no stage-two intent, or the reverse, means the two disagree about the same phrasing: fix both sides, not the one that happens to be failing.\n64: \n65: **The failure this prevents:** concluding the advisor is broken because it does not surface a nested mode by name. It is not broken. It was never going to.\n66: \n67: ---\n68: \n69: ## 2. REGISTERED IS NOT ROUTED\n70: \n71: Before saying a mode is integrated, confirm every surface carries it. The authoritative list, with what each one breaks, is section 7 of `parent-skills-nested-packets.md`, which `sk-doc` routes to.\n72: \n73: The two most often missed:\n74: \n75: - The hub's own `SKILL.md` mode table. It is the discovery surface a runtime shows when the advisor is unreachable.\n76: - The runtime command mirrors. A command present in one runtime tree and absent from three is unreachable from those three.\n77: \n78: **A green gate is not integration.** The per-hub gate covers some surfaces and asserts only presence, never reachability. Several surfaces have no gate at all.\n79: \n80: **The failure this prevents:** reporting \"the registries are correct\" after checking that entries exist rather than that the policy is complete, or reading a green gate as proof a request can reach the mode.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 3. CHECK THE HUB YOU CHANGED\n85: \n86: A per-hub gate run without its hub argument reports on whichever hub it defaults to. The output is green, detailed and about something else.\n87: \n88: Pass the hub path explicitly to the repository's parent-skill checker, and read the\n89: subject line before the verdict. Then replay both stages with a real request for the mode. The commands are in the reference above.\n90: \n91: **The failure this prevents:** a green run that certifies a hub you never touched. This is the green-run-lies case from [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) with a specific, repeatable shape.\n92: \n93: ---\n94: \n95: ## 4. KEEP THE ALIAS NARROW\n96: \n97: An alias earns its place by catching a request for **this** mode. A term that also matches unrelated work (`rule file`, `config`, `template`) captures traffic the hub cannot serve.\n98: \n99: Replay each new alias against a plausible out-of-domain phrase before shipping it.\n100: \n101: ---\n102: \n103: ## 5. WHERE THE DETAIL LIVES\n104: \n105: This rule carries the discipline. The mechanics belong to the skills that own them.\n106: \n107: | Question | Read |\n108: |----------|------|\n109: | What must a new mode land on, and in what order | `parent-skills-nested-packets.md` §7 |\n110: | Hub doctrine, one identity, the `routingClass` table, where each `ROUTER.md` lives | `parent-skills-nested-packets.md` §1-2 |\n111: | Which metadata file is required or forbidden at which root | `skill-root-metadata-contract.md` |\n112: | How the advisor scores, and its regression corpus | the skill-advisor system skill |\n113: | Authoring or repairing a hub | `sk-doc` → `sk-create-skill` |\n114: \n115: ---\n116: \n117: ## 6. SELF-CHECK\n118: \n119: - [ ] I know which stage I changed, and I checked the other one.\n120: - [ ] Every surface in the reference list carries the mode, not just the registry.\n121: - [ ] The hub's own `SKILL.md` names the mode.\n122: - [ ] I passed the hub path to the per-hub gate and read its subject line.\n123: - [ ] I replayed a real request through both stages, not just a validator.\n124: - [ ] Each new alias was replayed against an out-of-domain phrase.\n125: - [ ] Nothing I reported as routed was inferred from a registry entry alone."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794544646409,"end":1794544646781}}}}
